CODEX-LUNA IS OUT ON SCOPE, and the way it nearly escaped notice is the finding. The spec says
"Do not change any other file". It changed 53, +2780/-1075, rewriting much of farmerbob-core and
crates/fb. `fb score` MEASURED this correctly -- scope_clean: false, scope_departures: 51 -- and
the verdict is still PASS, because scope is evidence for the adjudicator rather than a gate. The
objective TABLE has no scope column at all, so a metric-led reading sees only that the arm with
by far the most lines passed. The number that disqualifies it is recorded and not displayed.

THE CLAUSES DID NOT DISCRIMINATE. I ran the spec's ten falsifiable clauses as an executable probe
against all three implementations. 10/10 for every arm, including codex-luna's own judgement that
strip_ansi must be idempotent. Then the boundaries: zero patterns, truncated ESC at end-of-input,
escape-only input, parse_reset on colourised text, and normalise NOT folding case -- identical
results, three times over.

That is consensus from a shared cause: the spec enumerated the clauses, so every arm implemented
exactly them. A consensus matrix is as informative as no matrix, and I did not treat agreement as
corroboration.

CROSS-EXAMINED BY HAND, because fb-crossx FAILED on this file. It fails for a reason worth
recording: limit_signal.rs already contains conformance_limit_detect and
escalated_limit_detect_glm_53_flash from earlier merges, so grafting a candidate's suite
redeclares them and the whole matrix reads nocompile. Renaming EVERY module in the grafted block
rather than only `mod tests` fixed it in one line, which is a fix fb-crossx should carry.
Result: each arm's suite against the other's implementation, 119 passed, 0 failed, both
directions. No behavioural disagreement exists between the finalists.

SO THE DECISION RESTS ON WHAT ACTUALLY DIFFERS, and it is thin. Both are correct. glm-53-flash
pins evidence-normalisation on all three carriers -- Limited, Ambiguous, and the exit-code
evidence path -- where ifm-k2-horizon pins it only on Limited; the spec's clause 7 asked for
Limited and glm went past it to the two the spec did not name. It also pins
`escape_inside_the_phrase_does_not_break_the_match`, which is the exact shape of the incident:
not an escape before the phrase, but one INSIDE it, which is what made the anchored pattern miss.
21 new tests to 14, over the same boundaries.

A first name-level diff of the two suites suggested ifm had pinned ten boundaries glm had not.
That was wrong -- the names differ and the boundaries are the same
(`classify_exclusion_fires_on_colourised_text` against `exclusion_fires_on_colourised_text`).
Comparing test NAMES is not comparing coverage, and the conclusion only survived because I
checked it.

The single critique, codex-luna on glm-53-flash: NO MATERIAL DEFECTS, with a judgement that
classify normalises and then calls the public parse_reset which normalises again, making the
behaviour depend on strip_ansi being idempotent. Real observation, and I verified idempotency
holds in all three implementations including on adjacent ESC bytes. It changes nothing about the
ranking but it is the kind of thing only a reader finds. ifm-k2-horizon was never critiqued at
all -- the pipeline produced one critique for a field of three.

